              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Premium Paddle Before Your Technique Is Consistent</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $280 paddle magnifies good technique; it does not create it. New players rarely feel the difference between a $100 raw carbon paddle and a premium model, and they often prefer the forgiveness of a cheaper, wider paddle. Spend the difference on lessons, balls and court shoes, then upgrade once you know your game.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Power When You Lose Points at the Kitchen</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many players buy a thin-core, elongated power paddle because drives feel good, then struggle to dink and reset. If most of your errors happen at the net, a 16mm core and a standard or hybrid shape will win you more points than extra pace from the baseline.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Grip Size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A grip that is too small makes you squeeze, which tires the forearm and can aggravate tennis elbow. A grip that is too large limits wrist action. Measure your hand, and if you are between sizes choose the smaller grip and build it up with an overgrip.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Every Paddle Is Tournament Legal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Not every paddle is on USA Pickleball's approved list, and approval can change. Before a sanctioned event, look up your exact model on the registry rather than relying on a retailer listing or the box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Reading the Warranty on a Thermoformed Paddle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thermoformed and unibody paddles have a widely reported risk of core crush or delamination. Warranty terms vary from a few months to a year or more. Read them before you buy, register the paddle if required, and keep the receipt.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

