          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best pickleball paddle for most players?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most intermediate players, a midweight paddle around 7.8 to 8.2 ounces with a 16mm core and a hybrid shape is the safest choice. The JOOLA Perseus Pro IV 16mm fits that description at the premium end, and the Vatic Pro Prism Flash 16mm offers a raw carbon face in the same mold for a listed $99.99.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 14mm or 16mm paddle better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better overall. A 16mm core gives more control, a larger sweet spot and more forgiveness, which suits most recreational players. A 14mm core returns energy faster for more pop on drives, which suits players who already have good control. If you are unsure, start with 16mm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are carbon fiber pickleball paddles worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most players who play regularly, yes. Carbon faces give a more consistent response, and raw carbon faces grip the ball for spin without relying on a grit coating that wears off. Raw carbon paddles now start around $100, so the premium over fiberglass is small.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much should I spend on a pickleball paddle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Beginners can play happily with a paddle or set under $100. Regular players get the best value between about $100 and $200, where raw carbon faces and modern construction are standard. Paddles above $200 are aimed at advanced players and signature-model buyers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What weight pickleball paddle should I get?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most players do best with a midweight paddle around 7.8 to 8.2 ounces. Lighter paddles are quicker and easier on the arm; heavier paddles are more stable and add power but tire the arm sooner. Swing weight, which depends on balance, matters as much as the static weight on the label.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a USA Pickleball approved paddle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only for sanctioned tournaments and some leagues. That said, most paddles from established brands are approved, and buying one keeps your options open. Check the exact model on USA Pickleball's online registry, because approval can change.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a pickleball paddle last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on how often you play and how the paddle is built. Regular players often notice a paddle losing grit or feeling dead after several months to a couple of years. Thermoformed paddles can also develop core crush, which makes them play hot and inconsistent. See our lifespan guide for the warning signs.
              </p>
